<Project>

  <!--
    Publish settings live in Directory.Build.targets, NOT Directory.Build.props.

    props is imported at the TOP of every project, before the project body has run, so a
    condition on a property the project sets (IsPublishableApp) is evaluated while that property
    is still empty and silently does nothing. The result is a publish that succeeds, produces a
    framework-dependent layout, and fails on every machine without the exact runtime installed —
    the same green-in-CI, broken-everywhere shape as a dropped self-contained flag, arrived at
    from the other direction. targets is imported at the BOTTOM, after the project body.

    Verify with: dotnet publish src/Parakeet.Cli -c Release -r win-x64 -o out
    A self-contained publish has hundreds of files. Eleven means this stopped working.
  -->
  <!--
    Gated on RuntimeIdentifier as well as IsPublishableApp. A plain `dotnet build` sets no RID, and
    a self-contained executable cannot be referenced by a non-self-contained one (NETSDK1151), so
    unconditionally setting it here would stop the test projects from referencing the CLI and the
    app at all. Anything with a RID is a real deployment and must be self-contained.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup Condition="'$(IsPublishableApp)' == 'true' and '$(RuntimeIdentifier)' != ''">
    <SelfContained>true</SelfContained>
    <PublishReadyToRun>true</PublishReadyToRun>
    <PublishReadyToRunComposite>false</PublishReadyToRunComposite>

    <!--
      Single-file is ON since 2026-08-23, and the reason it was off was wrong.

      What it said: "single-file extracts natives to a temp path that breaks the backend directory
      layout". That is what happens with IncludeNativeLibrariesForSelfExtract, which is off by
      default and which nothing here sets. Measured instead of assumed: the five native libraries
      that come from NuGet — SkiaSharp, HarfBuzz, ANGLE, ONNX Runtime — stay beside the executable,
      and native/<rid>/<backend>/ is untouched because those files are copied by
      build/NativeAssets.targets rather than bundled. Nothing extracts anywhere.

      What it buys, measured the same day by packing two versions with a one-line source change
      between them and reading the delta vpk produced:

        loose assemblies   84,365-byte delta,  209,965,214-byte full package, ~200 files
        single file        18,518-byte delta,  206,751,439-byte full package,   34 files

      So the update every user downloads is 4.6x smaller, not larger, which is the opposite of what
      was expected: a relinked 98.5 MB executable diffs well under zstd. Fewer loose assemblies
      beside the executable also means fewer things to side-load in its place.

      Trimming and NativeAOT stay off, and those reasons still hold: trimming cannot see through
      P/Invoke, and NativeAOT plus a hand-loaded native library is a debugging surface nobody needs.

      Two checks key on the shape of a publish and had to move with this — the file count and
      hostfxr.dll are gone from the output — see scripts/package-windows.ps1 and ci.yml, which now
      size the executable instead.
    -->
    <PublishSingleFile>true</PublishSingleFile>
    <PublishTrimmed>false</PublishTrimmed>
    <PublishAot>false</PublishAot>

    <SatelliteResourceLanguages>en</SatelliteResourceLanguages>
  </PropertyGroup>

</Project>
